# Unity C# Architecture Rules

Keep MonoBehaviours thin. Use MonoBehaviours for Unity lifecycle, serialized references, scene binding, input wiring, and visual updates.

Put deterministic logic into plain C# classes where practical.

Use shared services and stores for session, profile, settings, subjects, terms, quiz availability, quiz details, quiz session, quiz answer drafts, quiz validation, quiz submission, quiz results, safe errors, and navigation.

Do not put official scoring, answer keys, provider logic, or quiz business rules inside scene UI components.

Do not hardcode quiz content in UI scripts.

Do not hardcode future mission content.

Do not add unnecessary global singletons, service locators, or static mutable state.

Use explicit dependencies and small interfaces when they improve testing, provider swapping, or UI separation.

Preserve assembly boundaries. Runtime assemblies must not reference Editor assemblies.

Follow repository style first. Otherwise use PascalCase for types, methods, properties, and events; camelCase for locals and parameters; and _camelCase for private fields.

Prefer private serialized fields over public mutable fields for Inspector references.

Validate serialized references before use.

Subscribe and unsubscribe callbacks symmetrically.

Cancel async work when a screen closes, a scene unloads, a session ends, or a newer request replaces the old request.

Do not mutate UnityEngine objects from background threads.

Avoid scene-wide Find calls, repeated queries, avoidable allocations in hot paths, and unnecessary Update loops.

Use guard clauses to reduce nesting.

Do not silently swallow exceptions. Route safe user-facing errors through the safe error system and log developer details only where appropriate.
